.TH XUR.STD.BYTES 7 "2026-10-29" "xur 0.1.2" "Xur Manual"
.SH NAME
xur.std.bytes \- byte buffer operations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B use std::bytes;
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B std::bytes
creates byte buffers, and byte values have methods for reading and
writing integers at byte offsets in either endianness. Mutations happen
in place.
.SH MODULE FUNCTIONS
.TP
.B zeroed(len) -> bytes
A buffer of
.I len
zero bytes.
.TP
.B from_list(list) -> bytes
Convert a list of integers (0\(en255) to bytes.
.TP
.B from_hex(hex) -> Result<bytes, str>
Parse hex pairs, ignoring whitespace: "4d 5a".
.SH BYTE METHODS
.TP
.B len() -> i64
Length of the buffer.
.TP
.B is_empty() -> bool
Whether the buffer is empty.
.TP
.B push(byte) -> ()
Append a byte (0\(en255).
.TP
.B extend(other) -> ()
Append another byte buffer.
.TP
.B find(needle) -> Option<i64>
Find the first occurrence of
.I needle
in the buffer.
.TP
.B starts_with(prefix) -> bool
Whether the buffer starts with
.IR prefix .
.TP
.B to_hex() -> str
Format the buffer as a hex string.
.TP
.B decode() -> Result<str, str>
Decode the buffer as UTF-8.
.TP
.B to_list() -> [i64]
Convert each byte to an integer.
.SS Numeric reads and writes
Integers and floats are read and written at byte offsets. The suffix
indicates the byte order:
.B _le
for little-endian,
.B _be
for big-endian.
.B u8
and
.B i8
have no suffix (single byte).
.PP
Read:
.BR read_u8(off) ,
.BR read_i8(off) ,
.BR read_u16_le(off) ,
.BR read_u16_be(off) ,
.BR read_i16_le(off) ,
.BR read_i16_be(off) ,
.BR read_u32_le(off) ,
.BR read_u32_be(off) ,
.BR read_i32_le(off) ,
.BR read_i32_be(off) ,
.BR read_u64_le(off) ,
.BR read_u64_be(off) ,
.BR read_i64_le(off) ,
.BR read_i64_be(off) ,
.BR read_f32_le(off) ,
.BR read_f32_be(off) ,
.BR read_f64_le(off) ,
.BR read_f64_be(off) .
.PP
Write:
.BR write_u8(off,\ val) ,
.BR write_i8(off,\ val) ,
.BR write_u16_le(off,\ val) ,
.BR write_u16_be(off,\ val) ,
and so on for every read variant.
.SH STRING METHODS
Strings also have:
.TP
.B "text".to_bytes() -> bytes
Encode the string as UTF-8 bytes.
.TP
.B str::from_hex(hex) -> Result<bytes, str>
Same as
.BR bytes::from_hex .
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
use std::bytes;
use std::io;

fn main() {
    let buf = bytes::zeroed(8);
    buf.write_u32_le(0, 0x00905a4d);
    io::println("MZ header: {}", buf.to_hex());
    io::println("u32 at 0: {}", buf.read_u32_le(0));
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xur.std (7),
.BR xur.std.list (7)
